% The four binding kinds, in the positions M2 can use them.

### name: bind.column.typed
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "total", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T * 2
--- expect
(`o`.`total` * 2)
===
### name: bind.value.scalar-is-quoted-by-default
--- note
A host-supplied value is TEXT unless it says otherwise. Guessing from the
characters would emit a product code of "00123" as the number 123.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"CODE": {"kind": "value", "value": "00123"}}
--- source
CODE
--- expect
'00123'
===
### name: bind.value.numeric-when-declared
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"LIMIT": {"kind": "value", "value": "100", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
LIMIT
--- expect
100
===
### name: bind.value.indexed
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"L": {"kind": "value", "value": {"1": "a", "2": "b"}}}
--- source
L[2]
--- expect
'b'
===
### name: bind.value.list-is-not-a-scalar
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"L": {"kind": "value", "value": {"1": "a", "2": "b"}}}
--- source
L
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:1
===
### name: bind.columns.indexed-positionally
--- note
Keys are "1", "2", … exactly as `,` builds them.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"table": "x", "column": "a", "type": "NUM"}, {"table": "x", "column": "b", "type": "NUM"}]}}
--- source
V[2] > 0
--- expect
(`x`.`b` > 0)
===
### name: bind.columns.index-out-of-range
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"table": "x", "column": "a", "type": "NUM"}]}}
--- source
V[5]
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING 1:2
===
### name: bind.relation.field-is-a-column-reference
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"I": {"kind": "relation", "from": "order_items", "alias": "oi", "fields": {"QTY": {"table": "oi", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
I["qty"] > 0
--- expect
(`oi`.`qty` > 0)
### name: bind.value.num-must-already-be-canonical
--- note
This case used to expect `(7 + 1)`, and the reasoning it carried is still true:
the NUM branch emits what Dec::parse recovered, because proving a string is a
number and then emitting a different string is the gap "1 OR 1=1 -- " lived in.

What was missing is that the evaluator is handed the host's original text and
keeps it. So `007` translated to `7` while SEL kept "007", and the two agreed
on `X + 1` and disagreed on everything that reads a number as text: LEN was 1
against 3, TRIM was "7" against "007", `X $== "007"` was false against TRUE.
Emit::numericLiteral's docblock states the assumption exactly -- "the two agree
for everything the parser produces" -- and a value binding is not the parser.

Refusing is the only outcome that holds: canonicalising here would still
disagree with the evaluator, which never runs this code. The non-canonical set
is small and entirely leading zeros and negative zero.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "value", "value": "007", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
X + 1
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: bind.value.canonical-num-still-emits-bare
--- note
The other half: 2.50 round-trips, so it is unaffected. Scale is part of a SEL
number and survives.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "value", "value": "2.50", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
X + 1
--- expect
(2.50 + 1)
===
### name: bind.value.negative-zero-is-not-canonical
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "value", "value": "-0", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
X + 1
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: bind.value.untyped-non-canonical-is-fine
--- note
Without `type: NUM` the value is text, goes out quoted, and the characters the
host supplied are the characters SEL has. Nothing to refuse.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "value", "value": "007"}}
--- source
X & ""
--- expect
CONCAT('007', '')
===
### name: bind.value.empty-is-not-a-scalar
--- note
An empty result set as a value binding. Legal as the thing an aggregate
iterates, nothing as a value -- and asking for its characters used to raise a
SelError from inside asValue(), past tryTranslate(), which a host uses so it
does not need a try/catch.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "value", "value": []}}
--- source
X $== "a"
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: bind.value.empty-is-still-an-aggregate-source
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "value", "value": []}}
--- source
ALL(X, _ > 0)
--- expect
TRUE
===
### name: bind.column.nul-in-a-name-is-refused
--- note
Emit::ident doubles the quote character and passes everything else through,
which is right for every character but two. A NUL terminates the C string
libpq and sqlite3 are handed, so it is malformed SQL on all four servers; an
empty name quotes to "" , which PostgreSQL rejects and the others accept.
No dialect has an escape for either, and a column with a NUL in its name does
not exist.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "column", "column": "a\u0000b"}}
--- source
X
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: bind.column.array-column-is-refused
--- note
`(string)` on an array is the characters "Array", and `Array` is a legal
identifier -- so in a schema with a column of that name the wrong column is
read and nothing says so. The same audit reached relation `from` and
`correlate` and stopped there.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "column", "column": ["a", "b"]}}
--- source
X
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: bind.column.array-table-is-refused
--- note
Worse than "Array": this one reached Emit::column(?string) and raised a
TypeError, which is not a SqlError, so tryTranslate() returned a fatal instead
of null.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "column", "column": "c", "table": {"raw": "x"}}}
--- source
X
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: bind.relation.array-alias-is-refused
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "t", "alias": ["x"], "fields": {"Q": {"column": "q"}}}}
--- source
ALL(R, I, I["Q"] > 0)
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: bind.value.num-keeps-its-scale
--- note
Canonical is not the same as shortest: scale is part of a SEL number (spec §4.1)
and part of what SQL DECIMAL arithmetic reads, so 2.50 stays 2.50.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "value", "value": "2.50", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
X + 1
--- expect
(2.50 + 1)
===
### name: bind.relation.correlate-must-be-raw-string
--- note
`raw` is the one place a host writes SQL this layer cannot check the meaning of.
It can still check the shape, and must: (string) on an array yields "Array" plus
a warning, and that string is then spliced into a correlated subquery as if it
were a join condition.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "t", "alias": "r", "correlate": {"raw": {"mariadb": "1=1"}}, "fields": {"A": {"table": "r", "column": "a", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
COUNT(R)
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: bind.relation.from-array-must-be-raw-string
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": {"query": "select 1"}, "alias": "r", "fields": {"A": {"table": "r", "column": "a", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
COUNT(R)
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
